\DOC X86_ACCSTEPS_TAC

\TYPE {X86_ACCSTEPS_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> int list -> int list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes a block of x86 instruction steps on a goal
{A ?- eventually x86 P s<i>}, advancing the state variable, replacing the old
state's register/flag/memory assumptions with ones about the new state, and
recording selected arithmetic steps as accumulated carry/sum equations.

\DESCRIBE
{X86_ACCSTEPS_TAC th anums snums} takes {th : thm * (thm option array)}, the
execution/decoding data for the program being stepped (a length theorem paired
with a cache of per-instruction theorems), which fixes the code being
symbolically run; {anums : int list}, the step numbers to be treated as
accumulation steps, for which an explicit arithmetic equation relating the
carry bit and the sum word to the operands is added as an assumption; and
{snums : int list}, the full list of step numbers to execute (usually an
enumeration/range), determining how far the state is advanced.
{
       A, B(s<i>) ?- eventually x86 P s<i>
    ======================================= X86_ACCSTEPS_TAC th anums snums
    A, E1 ... En, B(s<j>) ?- eventually x86 P s<j>
}
\noindent Here {s<j>} is the state after the last requested step, and {P}
itself is unchanged up to simplification of its body. {B(s<i>)} is the set of
assumptions describing the starting state — the {bytes_loaded},
{read}-component and {MAYCHANGE} assumptions mentioning the old state variable;
these are discharged and replaced by the corresponding assumptions {B(s<j>)}
about the new state, including fresh {read} equations for every component
touched, flag assumptions, the updated program counter, and a combined
{MAYCHANGE} relation from the original state to the new one. {E1 ... En} are
the added accumulation equations (n may be zero), one for each step named in
{anums}, each expressing the word result and carry bit in terms of the inputs
(over {&}/{--(&2 pow 64)} arithmetic); multiplication steps may likewise add
high/low product equations, and fresh variables such as the per-step sums and
carries appear in these. Assumptions {A} not referring to the state variable
are generally preserved, though they may be reordered or re-added.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals about {eventually x86} with a state-describing assumption set.

\ENDDOC
